[[:db/retract 17592186120383 :question/answer-fields 17592186118412] [:db/retract 17592186120362 :question/answer-fields 17592186118286] {:db/id 17592186119823 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1819c1f8-4cd2-5652-80f1-d185af0479c5" "question-c92100bd-871b-5993-bed2-e255a4c7bcbb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119634 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](images/71/7138c7f0cf14978e4c345b91b7c5b5e38c4c74d00a83a5f9a0908249b5602045.svg) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -\\mathbf{b}_2$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = -3\\mathbf{b}_1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since ![](images/f2/f2c4bc20db558b0e0498da6c9b8653f8e093a7b3a517a21ec07803cdf3debf98.svg) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5{b}_{1}-2{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-2{b}_{2}+5{b}_{1} \\\\  & =2(-{b}_{2})-\\frac{5}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =2{c}_{1}-\\frac{5}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/f6/f6a8d41276d74ce726301f3cc83d87542d49115eb46cf0f836d20816cd660168.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119824 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-be777f36-f8c5-5b1e-a52c-750939157337"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119635 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and ![](images/19/191549b963b841cb97bbad9b6c65019d913cc148ed466d3c026cc27eccff1d8d.svg) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2, \\mathbf{c}_3 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = -3\\mathbf{b}_2,$ $\\mathbf{c}_2=-\\mathbf{b}_3,$ and $\\mathbf{c}_3 = -4\\mathbf{b}_1?$" :question/worked-solution "Since ![](images/33/334e52d3fd172cadf353bd6b3d6f3fe66eb0c722320da14ce6fd11b66cdca79a.svg) we can write $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1, \\mathbf c_2,$ and $\\mathbf c_3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3} \\\\  & =-3{b}_{2}+5{b}_{3}-2{b}_{1} \\\\  & =(-3{b}_{2})-5(-{b}_{3})+\\frac{1}{2}(-4{b}_{1}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}-5{c}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/9a/9a0eec63eca72981c016e4c504a49d249b6514e5e4f12e77e7d721282b7b24bb.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119825 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-07f1e9fd-ac52-515f-8ec2-7b10b57c07ec" "question-f46f0e34-3d5c-5e7a-9fb4-690993802dbe"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186119636 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1, \\mathbf{b}_2 \\right\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and ![](images/e8/e8c67f642148caf84c01b7ee88fe125c4a5ae37ecae313d01d45c277de51d74b.svg) What are the coordinates of $\\mathbf{x}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}= \\left\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1, \\mathbf{c}_2 \\right\\},$ where $\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\mathbf{b}_2-\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2 = 2\\mathbf{b}_1 - 3\\mathbf{b}_2?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}={b}_{2}-{b}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}=2{b}_{1}-3{b}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the $\\mathbf{b}$'s in terms of $\\mathbf{c}$'s. Multiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{c}_1+\\mathbf{c}_2 = -\\mathbf{b}_2\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_2 = -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{c}_1 = \\left( -2\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2 \\right) - \\mathbf{b}_1\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n\\mathbf{b}_1 = -3\\mathbf{c}_1-\\mathbf{c}_2.\n$$\n\nSince ![](images/0e/0e283c9948eacf3e231eef3a3feb355c33f20c3dbfd89e97c530331758af34ad.svg) we can write the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in terms of the vectors $\\mathbf c_1$ and $\\mathbf c_2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-3{b}_{2} \\\\  & =(-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2})-3(-2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}) \\\\  & =-3{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}+6{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\  & =3{c}_{1}+2{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/d8/d8d25ca46df4435c67e7c23e61851e2acd4826b0b2d99d9b54de8b6f2c37ee94.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-1819c1f8-4cd2-5652-80f1-d185af0479c5" :question/id #uuid "1819c1f8-4cd2-5652-80f1-d185af0479c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-35260" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].$ What are the coordinates of $x$ relative to the basis $C={{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ where ${c}_{1}=5{b}_{2}$ and ${c}_{2}=-3{b}_{1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]$, we can write $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={b}_{1}-5{b}_{2} \\\\  & =-5{b}_{2}+{b}_{1} \\\\  & =-(5{b}_{2})-(-{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =-(5{b}_{2})-\\frac{1}{3}(-3{b}_{1}) \\\\  & =-{c}_{1}-\\frac{1}{3}{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $[x]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d0279881-209b-5f19-932e-f90a7a749d9f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0279881-209b-5f19-932e-f90a7a749d9f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0279881-209b-5f19-932e-f90a7a749d9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5c547e71-e775-5595-94ae-8b1df8563107" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5c547e71-e775-5595-94ae-8b1df8563107" "answer-003997e3-71e3-5fd0-8716-b578a050327a" "answer-9e39fb95-43e2-541a-8451-cb5e1a8b5d30" "answer-54025de4-e25d-59ae-86a9-1ee27366fc6d" "answer-98bdd91b-9e6f-5202-91b7-08635ee7916f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c547e71-e775-5595-94ae-8b1df8563107" :answer/id #uuid "5c547e71-e775-5595-94ae-8b1df8563107" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/valu